WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.970
QUESTION: So what I really want to take from your
research is an idea that this

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:11.969
pro-democracy nationalism inoculates
against the anti-democracy

00:00:11.969 --> 00:00:17.880
nationalism. But when I think of the
riots in Gujarat, which happened 20 years

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:22.320
after independence, and five years before the time period you're talking

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:29.939
about, and the rise of the BJP today, I
can't take away that message. So

00:00:29.939 --> 00:00:34.590
what is the message that I can
take away about the difference and the

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.950
interplay of these two types of
nationalisms? MAYA TUDOR: Yeah, it's a great question.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:45.870
MARGARET LEVI:  Much better said than what I was
trying to get at. TUDOR: Right, so I guess what I

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:52.530
would say is that the word "inoculates" is
far too strong. All the explanations that

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:55.530
political scientists have for when
democracy does or doesn't come about,

00:00:55.530 --> 00:01:02.940
they're all probabilistic explanations.
They're not—nothing, not a super

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:08.100
diverse ethnic group, a super high level
of economic development,

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:14.130
Nothing is absolutely sufficient to become a
democracy. So the claim that I'm making

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:18.119
is that, all else being equal, and of
course, nothing else is ever equal, right?

00:01:18.119 --> 00:01:23.790
Because countries are very particular
space-and-time

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:30.380
-specific creations, but that all else
being equal, a relatively flat and open

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:36.060
porous conception of what it means to be
a member of that nation is a resource

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:42.509
for democracy because of subsequent
moments of political crisis, when you

00:01:42.509 --> 00:01:49.409
have political entrepreneurs pushing to
get to power. If there is by

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:55.409
contrast a narrative of nation that
specifically sets aside a

00:01:55.409 --> 00:02:00.600
group as not being core to defining the
nation, that in moments of crisis, that

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:05.219
particular group can be more easily
targeted by politicians in power. And

00:02:05.219 --> 00:02:11.720
that's what you see happening with
Muslims, the Rohingya in Myanmar,

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:19.280
it's what you see today happening
in India, and what you saw when Modi was

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.310
the Chief Minister of Gujarat, which is
when the riots that you mentioned

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:27.410
happened. He's very much been pushing a
Hindu nationalist vision. And it's not a

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:32.060
coincidence that's exactly where you saw
the riots happening in India, because

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:38.330
it's where the chief politician in power
was pushing a majoritarian view of what

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:42.860
it meant to be a citizen of that nation.
So to go back to your question, what do I

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:46.670
take away from this talk? It's not that
anything inoculates democracy.

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:50.230
Democracy is only as strong as our
belief in it and our willingness to

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:54.950
essentially make sacrifices on behalf of
a particular way of organizing and

00:02:54.950 --> 00:03:01.400
sharing power. But what I can say is that
in addition to all those structural

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:05.480
factors which do make democracy a little
bit more likely. So if you're a little

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:09.080
bit wealthier, a little bit more likely.
If you don't have oil and gas as a

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:13.040
major part of your export, democracy is a
little more likely. And what I want to

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:17.030
add to the conversation is when you have
a nationalism that's relatively

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:22.200
egalitarian, it makes democracy a little
more likely for the reasons I mentioned.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:25.400
LEVI: Except that in all these cases you have both kinds of nationalism pervading.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:31.340
TUDOR: Right. So I think that's another...so just briefly, I think that

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:35.360
that was a question I was sure
I was gonna get. "You know, when

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.790
you're talking about one nationalism, but
there are lots of kinds of nationalisms."

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:43.910
And I think that's absolutely true.
Founding moments provide particularly

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:48.290
powerful narratives of what the nation
is, but of course those narratives are

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:53.420
contested and changing over time. But they're often surprisingly

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:58.160
durable. And what you see happening
in India right now is a sustained

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:02.810
attempt to change what the national
identity is, and to the extent that it

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:07.459
endures, it's in power, it begins to change
national curriculums, which, you know,

00:04:07.459 --> 00:04:14.080
is exactly what we're seeing, that will
become a resource for essentially

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:17.780
promoting one form of democratic
breakdown just when those civil and

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:24.570
political liberties are systematically
denied to the out groups.

